one of the most powerful beings in the  Marvel Cinematic Universe is on her way  to the big screen if you're unfamiliar  with the latest MCU headliner Carole  Captain Marvel Danvers here are a few  basic facts you're gonna want to keep in  mind as you settle in with your popcorn  Captain Marvel tells the story of Carol  Danvers played by Oscar winner brie  Larson unlike most MCU flicks the movie  isn't set in the present day but instead  takes place in the 90s  this gives Marvel plenty of time to show  Danvers becoming Captain Marvel and  explain why she was nowhere to be found  as the events of 2008 Ironman assured  him the MCU superhero age and why she  wasn't even referenced until the  post-credits scenes in 2018 infinity war  Danvers was first introduced as a  character in 1968 Marvel superheroes  number 13 she was a retired Air Force  pilot serving a supporting role for the  character Marvel who had been created a  year earlier and was then occupying the  role of Captain Marvel cutting out  decades of complicated comic-book  backstory the MCU seems to have decided  to streamline Danvers origins as they've  done more than once for the screen  versions of their classic characters in  the past one of the central forces in  Captain Marvel is a group that entered  the Marvel Comics universe way back in  1967 in Fantastic Four number 65 we're  talking about the Kree a technologically  advanced race of aliens that generally  take the form of blue skinned and purple  eyed beings with otherwise human  features they aren't necessarily bad  guys but they tend to have a bit of a  mixed reputation as they can be  militaristic and are infamous for their  constant squabbles around the galaxy  Danvers herself became a human crew  Hydra due to an accident that altered  her DNA and you're free a race of noble  warriors heroes noble warrior heroes but  the movie actually won't be the kree's  first foray into the MCU the ravager  yondu from guardians of the galaxy for  example revealed that he was sold to the  Kree in his youth Ronan the accuser was  also a rogue creed general that ignored  the treaty between his people and the  Nova Corps and attacked Xander with the  help of Thanos the blue skinned aliens  have also made a number of appearances  on ABC's agents of shield  while the Cree have a bit of a mixed  history their longtime enemies the  extraterrestrial race known as the  scrolls are far less complicated at  least as far as humans are concerned  their appearances in the comics usually  correlate with earth invasions or their  more distant and ancient quarrels with  the Kree the scrolls first appeared in  the comics in Fantastic Four number two  in 1962 making them slightly more  well-established than Danvers or the  Kree the most important thing you're  going to want to remember about the  scrolls is that they're shapeshifters  able to take on whatever appearance they  need in order to infiltrate and destroy  empires organizations and even herds of  superheroes causing distress and  distrust from the inside out this of  course means they could already be  involved in more than one MCU movie  infiltrating high positions and simply  biding their time for the perfect moment  to strike  and you thought Hydra was sneaky another  element of Carol Danvers Captain Marvel  comic book history is her ability to  take on her so-called binary form which  first appeared in the uncanny x-men  number 164 Danvers ability to transform  into the super-powered binary emerged  when yet another evil alien race known  as the brood abducted and experimented  on her further augmenting her already  significantly altered DNA as binary  Danvers could transform into an energy  manipulating state that allowed her to  lift more than 100 tons and fly it  nearly the speed of light and these  skills seemed to be in evidence in the  trailers in the third Captain Marvel  trailer we get a quick glimpse of Carol  suited up and leading a group of aliens  all dressed in 